Title:
VPN Phishing (Windows-native SSTP tunnel for quiet initial access)

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post describes an initial access technique where a target Windows workstation is tricked into establishing an outbound VPN (SSTP over 443) to an attacker-controlled server, allowing the operator to join the same tunnel from a second endpoint and reach the workstation over SMB/WinRM/RDP. The goal is to avoid traditional “malware/C2” signals by using Microsoft-signed VPN components and OS-native APIs instead of beacons, reverse shells, or custom network transports. It covers practical constraints: the access is limited to the single host, admin elevation is needed to switch the VPN profile from Public to Private (firewall), and SSTP requires a trusted TLS certificate to avoid prompts. The setup uses a server-side script to provision the VPN and generate a client stager (VBS/JS/PS1) that creates/dials the VPN via rasphone/Add-VpnConnection/RASAPI32. It also details creating a “hidden” local admin user by writing directly to the SAM registry hive (usernames ending with `$` are filtered by common tools) to avoid typical account-creation telemetry like Event ID 4720. Useful for red teams/pentesters focused on stealthy initial access and defenders interested in detecting abuse of Windows VPN and SAM manipulation.

Technical Focus:
- SSTP VPN over HTTPS (TCP/443) with trusted certificates
- Windows native VPN creation/dialing (rasphone, Add-VpnConnection, RASAPI32)
- Network profile category switching (Public → Private) and firewall implications
- SAM registry hive manipulation for stealth local account creation
- In-memory execution of C# assemblies via PowerShell (Assembly.Load)
- Post-connection remote management over SMB/WinRM/RDP (e.g., NetExec/Impacket)

Use Cases:
- Establishing a low-noise foothold without deploying a traditional C2 implant
- Bypassing restrictive egress controls by tunneling via SSTP over 443
- Gaining remote admin access to a single workstation via SMB/WinRM/RDP after user-assisted elevation
- Creating stealthy local admin accounts for persistence on a compromised endpoint
- Blue-team threat hunting for anomalous VPN profile creation + SAM hive writes

Keywords:
SSTP, VPN phishing, Windows VPN, rasphone, Add-VpnConnection, RASAPI32, TLS certificate chain, Let’s Encrypt, UAC prompt, network category Private/Public, Windows Firewall, SMB, WinRM, RDP, WireGuard, PowerShell stager, in-memory C# assembly, SAM hive, HKLM\\SAM\\SAM\\Domains\\Account\\Users, Event ID 4720, hidden local user account, NetExec, Impacket